your book has 12 chapters, of which 11 focus on a specific anti-pattern.

13
00:00:59,990 --> 00:01:05,509
we will be delving into some of those anti-patterns, but i wanted to talk about high level.

14
00:01:05,509 --> 00:01:07,990
why did you choose to focus on how things go wrong?

15
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,230
so that was actually pretty organic process throughout the writing of the book.

16
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:22,630
so the inspiration for the book was originally writing a devops book for people that aren't in ideal situations.

17
00:01:22,630 --> 00:01:28,950
so a lot of the material that was out there was great for small organizations.

18
00:01:28,950 --> 00:01:31,830
or was written for c-suite executives that had the power to restructure the org chart.

19
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:35,270
and make all these broad sweeping changes.

20
00:01:35,270 --> 00:01:38,789
but there were a lot of individual contributors and lower level managers.

21
00:01:38,789 --> 00:01:41,830
that i spoke with out in conference circles and things like that.

22
00:01:41,830 --> 00:01:47,749
where they wanted this change, but didn't feel empowered to be able to do it or make it.

23
00:01:47,759 --> 00:01:51,350
so that was originally the genesis of the book.

24
00:01:51,350 --> 00:01:56,870
and then throughout the writing process, we found that it was just a little more engaging.

25
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:00,709
and entertaining to establish these sort of anti-patterns.

26
00:02:00,709 --> 00:02:02,870
because they resonated with people so quickly.

27
00:02:02,870 --> 00:02:07,350
and it was a very good hook for them to say like, yes i feel this pain.

28
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:10,309
i understand this pain, but i may not understand where it's coming from.

29
00:02:10,309 --> 00:02:12,710
or what the sort of genesis of that is.

30
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:18,710
so you know, we noodled around with that idea a bit, and it really started to work out.

31
00:02:18,710 --> 00:02:20,550
so that's kind of how the approach came about.

32
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:25,030
you may have just answered this, or perhaps you can expand on it a bit.

33
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:32,309
these anti-patterns, when they exist in organizations, are they considered pain points?

34
00:02:32,309 --> 00:02:36,550
or is it simply that's how we do things, and people don't think there must be a better way?

35
00:02:36,550 --> 00:02:40,150
depends on your perspective, usually.

36
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:44,869
sometimes anti-patterns exist for the benefit of one group at the detriments of the other, right?

37
00:02:44,869 --> 00:02:47,910
so if you're on one side of the spectrum, you may just say oh, this is just business as usual.

38
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:54,470
but if you're the one sort of being crushed by this, you know, you're much more apt.

39
00:02:54,470 --> 00:02:57,030
to want to dig into the causes, and actually fix this thing.

40
00:02:57,030 --> 00:03:03,509
so for example, from a production operations perspective, it may feel very normal.

41
00:03:03,509 --> 00:03:05,430
and natural that devs have zero access to production.

42
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:08,470
because devs break things, they just can't be trusted.

43
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:11,910
but from a dev perspective, they're like, well, there's a lot of things i can't do.

44
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:15,830
in a day-to-day basis, that would be much better handled.

45
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:19,270
and much more direct and effective, if i were able to do that, right?

46
00:03:19,270 --> 00:03:22,630
so your perspective on if that's an anti-pattern or not differs.

47
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:26,789
but i think if you step back and look at the health of the organization organizationally.

48
00:03:26,789 --> 00:03:30,470
people will look and say like, you know what, this is not completely effective.

49
00:03:30,470 --> 00:03:33,350
the way we're doing it, and the truth really lives somewhere in the middle.

50
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:36,470
so how do we go about, you know, getting the two sides together?

51
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:40,149
and you know, solving this problem, which is really a common problem.

52
00:03:40,149 --> 00:03:46,790
because you know, the operation staff doesn't want to be tasked with constantly doing things. that they're not adding value for.

53
00:03:46,799 --> 00:03:51,030
and developers don't want to be stuck waiting behind some arbitrary gate.

54
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:54,710
for someone to type a bunch of commands that they're going to dictate.

55
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:58,470
and the person typing the commands may or may not fully understand what they're typing anyways.

56
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:03,110
perhaps you're familiar with famous quote from russian literature.

57
00:04:03,110 --> 00:04:09,750
that all happy families are alike, but every unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.

58
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:17,990
do all organizations have the same issues, or does everyone have a unique set of problems?

59
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,430
so you know, this is definitely a point of, you know, controversy.

60
00:04:23,430 --> 00:04:28,629
i feel like most organizations, more often than not, have the same problems, right?

61
00:04:28,629 --> 00:04:33,830
it might be spiced a little bit differently, but you know, at the core, it's the same problems.

62
00:04:33,830 --> 00:04:39,670
and when we talk about that, you know, i'm really talking about the larger aggregate.

63
00:04:39,670 --> 00:04:41,830
of companies that we don't see in blog post every day, right?

64
00:04:41,830 --> 00:04:44,629
google has its own set of problems, apple has its own set of problems.

65
00:04:44,639 --> 00:04:46,469
netflix has its own set of problems.

66
00:04:46,479 --> 00:04:50,710
but because they have such an outsized voice in the industry.

67
00:04:50,710 --> 00:04:53,030
we forget like how uniquely rare those companies are.

68
00:04:53,040 --> 00:05:00,550
for every single netflix, right, there's a thousand local health insurance companies.

69
00:05:00,550 --> 00:05:02,310
that are trying to process claims on a daily basis.

70
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:06,310
so i think as an industry, we sort of give this outsize weight.

71
00:05:06,310 --> 00:05:09,430
to these companies that do have unique problems due to their scale.

72
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:13,430
but if we step back a little bit, and just ask like what folks are doing day to day.

73
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:17,270
i'm guessing most of us have, you know, a lot of the same problems.

74
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:21,749
there is a whole genre of books, and i'm not sure if you'd put yards in this or not.

75
00:05:21,759 --> 00:05:23,909
but they're called the xyz cookbook.

76
00:05:23,919 --> 00:05:31,189
where i look up, okay, i want to format a date string in python. i open the chapter to that, here's how to do it.

77
00:05:31,199 --> 00:05:34,150
your book does read a bit like that.

78
00:05:34,150 --> 00:05:41,350
in that i have this problem, let's understand what it is, look at some ways to fix it.

79
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:51,830
is devops transformation, if i identify all the pain points and fix them one at a time, am i good?

80
00:05:51,830 --> 00:05:57,671
or is there a bigger message or philosophy that underlies all of these issues? that needs to be grasped?

81
00:05:57,680 --> 00:05:59,830
i think there's an underlying philosophy.

82
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:05,990
and even if you look at the book, you'll see themes sort of reoccurring over and over again.

83
00:06:05,990 --> 00:06:09,670
in that you know, one of the big things that we just have to get better at.

84
00:06:09,670 --> 00:06:13,350
is understanding the actual problem that we're trying to solve.

85
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:18,230
and communicating amongst all of the stakeholders. to make sure that that's what we're solving for.

86
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:23,510
as opposed to sort of, you know, dealing with these past grievances or fiefdoms.

87
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:28,150
you know, that's sort of what continues to fuel the beast for lack of a better term.

88
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:31,510
so i do believe that there is a lot to be gained.

89
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:38,870
simply by taking some of the core principles of you know, how do we identify a problem. and how do we work together to solve it.

90
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:41,350
and applying those in any organization.

91
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:43,909
you know, common goal setting is a perfect example.

92
00:06:43,919 --> 00:06:50,390
there aren't too many organizations that can't benefit from having a common set of goals.

93
00:06:50,390 --> 00:06:52,790
and mobilizing a team to work towards that single goal.

94
00:06:52,790 --> 00:06:57,110
when you look at like the okr strategy that was popularized by google.

95
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:00,230
that's really what it is at the core, right?

96
00:07:00,230 --> 00:07:02,790
this is the goal that we're trying to accomplish.

97
00:07:02,790 --> 00:07:07,350
we want to make sure that all the teams are focused and working towards that goal.

98
00:07:07,350 --> 00:07:10,390
so if that can work, and you know, have impact on a company the size of google.

99
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:14,230
imagine what it can do for smaller organizations.

100
00:07:14,230 --> 00:07:16,870
as long as we're all rallying around the same piece.

101
00:07:16,870 --> 00:07:20,309
and i think a lot of the riff that has occurred between dev and ops.

102
00:07:20,309 --> 00:07:23,350
has, you know, historically been because there's a separate set of goals.

103
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:28,309
a separate set of metrics, and the two aren't forced to overlap in any way.

104
00:07:28,309 --> 00:07:30,469
that brings about meaningful collaboration.

105
00:07:30,479 --> 00:07:32,950
and in fact just sort of fuels animosity.

106
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:41,189
then these anti-patterns are instances, and then by understanding the pieces.

107
00:07:41,189 --> 00:07:43,990
you can understand the bigger picture, right?

108
00:07:43,990 --> 00:07:49,029
absolutely, because like you know, as engineers we're accustomed to seeing patterns of problems, right?

109
00:07:49,039 --> 00:07:54,869
even when you're going through school, you're not dealing with the actual real world problem.

110
00:07:54,869 --> 00:07:56,869
that you're going to experience once you graduate, and you're in the field.

111
00:07:56,879 --> 00:08:00,550
you're experiencing and dealing with an abstraction of that pattern.

112
00:08:00,550 --> 00:08:07,270
but then being taught on how to recognize and say oh, you know, this is just another manifestation of that problem.

113
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:12,230
once you begin to recognize those patterns, you start to see it even in human interactions.

114
00:08:12,240 --> 00:08:17,589
it's something that, you know, like i always talk about when i'm learning a technology.

115
00:08:17,589 --> 00:08:19,589
if you learn the underlying technology that it's based on.

116
00:08:19,599 --> 00:08:22,790
learning the particular implementations are really easy, right?

117
00:08:22,790 --> 00:08:27,589
if you understand how relational databases work, whether it's postgres or mysql or sql server.

118
00:08:27,599 --> 00:08:30,150
you know that there's going to be a transaction log.

119
00:08:30,150 --> 00:08:34,230
you know that there's going to be some sort of buffer pool, right?

120
00:08:34,230 --> 00:08:36,870
and the syntax and names around those things are just sort of flavor text for that implementation.

121
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:40,070
i think it's the same thing with organizations, right?

122
00:08:40,070 --> 00:08:43,509
once you understand the sort of patterns that you're accustomed to seeing.

123
00:08:43,509 --> 00:08:47,750
that fail in an organization, it's quick to identify and come up with remedies.

124
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:53,590
jeff, we've been talking in general high level for a bit here.

125
00:08:53,590 --> 00:08:58,310
i want to now talk about some of these anti-patterns you cover.

126
00:08:58,310 --> 00:09:00,150
as time permits, in the remaining part of the hour.

127
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:03,750
let's start out with one known as information hoarding.

128
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:06,870
also known as only brent nose.

129
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:09,829
can you explain what this is?

130
00:09:09,829 --> 00:09:15,030
so brent was a reference to the very popular character in the phoenix project book.

131
00:09:15,030 --> 00:09:19,910
where he is that resource that knows everything in the organization, right?

132
00:09:19,910 --> 00:09:24,710
and from the technical perspective and when things go bad. it's like, oh, brent always fixes that.

133
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:30,070
and i think every organization has had, or has a brent in their midst, right?

134
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:32,870
sometimes it might even be someone that's listening to this podcast right now.

135
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:39,110
and we've historically assumed that like brent could be an information hoarder.

136
00:09:39,110 --> 00:09:47,750
as a means of survival, right, as a means of self-worth. but in reality, it's usually a little bit more nuanced, right?

137
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:50,710
it's a combination of things.

138
00:09:50,710 --> 00:09:53,829
it's time to disseminate that information, it's who do you disseminate it to.

139
00:09:53,839 --> 00:09:57,350
so i wanted to create this chapter to sort of highlight the idea.

140
00:09:57,350 --> 00:10:02,150
that you could be an accidental information hoarder, all right?

141
00:10:02,150 --> 00:10:05,750
internally, you don't see yourself as sort of keeping all of this information personal and close to you.

142
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:09,190
but you're not actually doing anything to get the information out of your head.

143
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:11,910
and into the broader audience, and into the broader community.

144
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:15,509
so first, you got to recognize that you've got a problem. before you can actually solve it.

145
00:10:15,519 --> 00:10:17,509
and i'm guilty of this too, you know.

146
00:10:17,509 --> 00:10:21,910
you do a thing so often that, you know, you just, you might forget to document it.

147
00:10:21,910 --> 00:10:24,790
or you might forget to share it, or you might have the curse of the expert.

148
00:10:24,790 --> 00:10:28,949
where you just assume that everyone knows how to do this. oh yeah, of course you guys know how to do this.

149
00:10:28,949 --> 00:10:32,710
you simply do that, do that, run in this command, then boom. you know, bob's your uncle.

150
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:37,509
so that chapter was really about focusing on different ways and techniques.

151
00:10:37,509 --> 00:10:41,190
you can do to sort of get that information out into the world.

152
00:10:41,190 --> 00:10:44,790
out into your teams, so that you aren't the single point of contact.

153
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:48,949
i think another interesting facet of that too is the idea.

154
00:10:48,949 --> 00:10:55,190
that if you are that source of truth, every question that comes to you.

155
00:10:55,190 --> 00:11:01,269
the answer is always filtered through your own perspective, right?

156
00:11:01,269 --> 00:11:04,710
so if someone asks me a question that only i have information or data about.

157
00:11:04,720 --> 00:11:11,269
the answer that they receive is inherently going to be soiled by my perspective. which could be limiting, right?

158
00:11:11,279 --> 00:11:15,509
maybe i don't have the full context of things.

159
00:11:15,509 --> 00:11:17,110
and i'm self-selecting information that i think is helpful.

160
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:20,230
and leaving out other bits of information that i think are not.

161
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:23,509
but who knows, it could be, you know, the thing that breaks the case wide open.

162
00:11:23,509 --> 00:11:25,509
for whoever i'm explaining it to.

163
00:11:25,519 --> 00:11:32,389
hoarding sounds like psychological disorder or anti-social behavior.

164
00:11:32,389 --> 00:11:36,710
like those cat ladies who have 25 cats.

165
00:11:36,710 --> 00:11:40,630
or people who own every issue of a newspaper they subscribe to for 50 years.

166
00:11:40,630 --> 00:11:45,190
is it really a disorder, or is this a natural process?

167
00:11:45,190 --> 00:11:46,949
because people have expertise in different things.

168
00:11:46,949 --> 00:11:51,510
and they're driven to meet deliverables, you know. i think it's a mix of things.

169
00:11:51,510 --> 00:11:57,190
i think it is sort of like natural for humans to want to continue to collect stuff, right?

170
00:11:57,200 --> 00:12:00,230
and you know, to continue to accumulate.

171
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:05,190
whether it be knowledge, wealth, toys, you know, whatever.

172
00:12:05,190 --> 00:12:07,509
there's just this sort of inclination to always want more.

173
00:12:07,509 --> 00:12:10,790
and i don't know how much of that is a byproduct of society.

174
00:12:10,800 --> 00:12:14,150
and how much of it is just, you know, human nature.

175
00:12:14,150 --> 00:12:18,629
so i think it can quickly delve into the realm of disorder.

176
00:12:18,629 --> 00:12:20,629
just like anything where if you sort of take it too far.

177
00:12:20,629 --> 00:12:27,350
and i think that's how we have sort of characterized historically. this idea of information hoarding.

178
00:12:27,350 --> 00:12:35,350
that someone is doing it deliberately, and specifically to amass some level of, you know, power, influence, whatever.

179
00:12:35,350 --> 00:12:39,910
but i think that that's more frequently than not a um, a rarity.

180
00:12:39,920 --> 00:12:45,269
i have observed it can be really difficult to get engineers to write documentation.

181
00:12:45,279 --> 00:12:49,509
possibly it's many people's least favorite tasks.

182
00:12:49,509 --> 00:12:53,590
why is it hard to get people to document things?

183
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:57,110
it's hard because like you know, writing is hard.

184
00:12:57,120 --> 00:13:03,750
and it may not be someone's preferred method of communication.

185
00:13:03,750 --> 00:13:06,310
so i think that plays a big part in it, right?

186
00:13:06,310 --> 00:13:13,030
because to be able to sit down and write something in a way that is coherent and makes sense. one, it takes a lot of energy.

187
00:13:13,030 --> 00:13:15,590
that's something me and my wife joke about a lot, right?

188
00:13:15,590 --> 00:13:21,269
if you want to write a well-crafted email to a series of engineers or executives or whatever.

189
00:13:21,269 --> 00:13:23,190
it's not something you just sit down and bang out in five or ten minutes, right?

190
00:13:23,200 --> 00:13:26,710
like you really need to put thought through it.

191
00:13:26,710 --> 00:13:30,150
you need to make sure you're telling a story that is, you know, engaging someone.

192
00:13:30,150 --> 00:13:32,870
so that they're not looking at the subject line.

193
00:13:32,870 --> 00:13:35,910
and then just, you know, immediately sort of moving on.

194
00:13:35,910 --> 00:13:40,069
and filing it away to read later in the depths of email.

195
00:13:40,069 --> 00:13:42,629
so i think writing is a lot harder than we actually give it credit for.

196
00:13:42,629 --> 00:13:45,670
and then writing good documentation is harder than we give it credit for.

197
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:49,670
and we don't really teach people how to write good documentation, right?

198
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:54,870
it's sort of assumed that, you know, oh, well, you know, you went to an english class.

199
00:13:54,870 --> 00:13:56,710
therefore you know how to write good technical documentation.

200
00:13:56,710 --> 00:14:00,069
no, it's an entire discipline, right?

201
00:14:00,069 --> 00:14:02,710
like we have people whose entire job is to write technical documentation.

202
00:14:02,720 --> 00:14:07,910
yet for some reason we're going to give this task to an engineer. and just assume that they're going to know how to do it.

203
00:14:07,920 --> 00:14:13,269
so like anything, if you're not particularly good at it, you don't like to do it.

204
00:14:13,269 --> 00:14:15,110
but then it's also like how is it valued in the organization?

205
00:14:15,120 --> 00:14:20,949
is it something that is actually prioritized, and given agency?

206
00:14:20,949 --> 00:14:23,269
if you are giving me, you know, 10 assignments.

207
00:14:23,269 --> 00:14:25,829
and then on top of that 10th assignment you say also write documentation.

208
00:14:25,839 --> 00:14:30,470
well guess what, in my list of mental priorities. documentation is always going to be at the bottom.

209
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:33,910
and if you keep shoving things on top of that, well then, you know.

210
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:41,269
you are sort of silently communicating where documentation exists in the value chain in the organization.

211
00:14:41,269 --> 00:14:45,670
so i think as leaders in the organization need to make sure that they're prioritizing documentation.

212
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:49,110
the same way they prioritize other work, right?

213
00:14:49,110 --> 00:14:51,350
if you're doing a sprint type of workflow.

214
00:14:51,350 --> 00:14:58,069
you know, there needs to be a ticket that says like, hey, write some documentation around process x. and that work needs to be accounted for.

215
00:14:58,079 --> 00:15:01,829
too often we, you know, leave it to the fringes of the work week.

216
00:15:01,839 --> 00:15:05,269
to try to get that documentation done.

217
00:15:05,279 --> 00:15:09,509
the concept of service ownership is gaining a lot of traction.

218
00:15:09,519 --> 00:15:12,949
where the people who built the app run it.

219
00:15:12,949 --> 00:15:16,550
you may be on a rotation for on call.

220
00:15:16,550 --> 00:15:20,310
and you may be at an hour where other team members are not awake.

221
00:15:20,310 --> 00:15:26,949
you need to fix possibly a range of different things that might occur on your service. including things you didn't built.

222
00:15:26,959 --> 00:15:33,829
how important is documentation for the on-call and response process?

223
00:15:33,829 --> 00:15:37,509
it's important, but even more important is that it's accurate.

224
00:15:37,519 --> 00:15:44,150
and in the middle of the night, there is nothing more dangerous in my world.

225
00:15:44,150 --> 00:15:46,069
than incorrect old outdated documentation.

226
00:15:46,069 --> 00:15:50,069
because you can make a ton of really bad decisions based off of that.

227
00:15:50,079 --> 00:15:58,389
so i feel it's extremely important to have something to have someone to go or make a decision off of.

228
00:15:58,389 --> 00:16:01,749
but i think we also need to be cognizant of what that means.

229
00:16:01,749 --> 00:16:05,110
in terms of our responsibility to keep that documentation up to date.

230
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:11,030
so if you're in a scenario where you don't know if this documentation has been updated frequently.

231
00:16:11,030 --> 00:16:16,629
you're probably going to end up paging out to that other person's team anyways, right?

232
00:16:16,629 --> 00:16:23,430
so there is a more holistic decision around what documentation looks like in those worlds.

233
00:16:23,430 --> 00:16:25,350
and how it impacts on call.

234
00:16:25,350 --> 00:16:28,310
there may be documentation that specifically says if there's a problem with this system.

235
00:16:28,310 --> 00:16:33,590
you just need to page out, because it changes too frequently, right?

236
00:16:33,590 --> 00:16:35,350
and that's another common complaint i hear about documentation.

237
00:16:35,360 --> 00:16:39,670
you know, things change so frequently that we just can't keep up with the documentation.

238
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:42,870
and then suddenly, you know, we've got this really outdated piece.

239
00:16:42,870 --> 00:16:47,189
so maybe the documentation is just say, you know, this is the on-call schedule.

240
00:16:47,189 --> 00:16:48,710
to page out to if there's an issue here.

241
00:16:48,720 --> 00:16:50,230
you know, it's a delicate balancing act.

242
00:16:50,240 --> 00:16:54,949
especially with these service oriented teams where they sort of got carte blanche.

243
00:16:54,949 --> 00:16:57,910
to iterate on a thing as frequently as possible.

244
00:16:57,910 --> 00:17:01,910
because they don't necessarily have the ripple effect or the the impact.

245
00:17:01,910 --> 00:17:03,590
of, you know, contributing to a huge monolith.

246
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:06,710
where there's 10 or 12 cooks in the kitchen.

247
00:17:06,720 --> 00:17:11,189
we've talked about people may not want to write documentation.

248
00:17:11,189 --> 00:17:13,110
or feel like they have time, or be good at it.

249
00:17:13,120 --> 00:17:17,990
in the agile methodology, you have this concept, definition of done.

250
00:17:17,990 --> 00:17:25,189
which is when i say my task is done, what exactly am i saying that i did?

251
00:17:25,189 --> 00:17:30,230
and you could add documentation as one of the components of definition of done.

252
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:36,789
is that going to be an effective strategy for really getting the documentation done?

253
00:17:36,789 --> 00:17:44,549
so again, i think that boils down to the organization, right? the fixes for documentation can be relatively easy.

254
00:17:44,549 --> 00:17:47,909
as long as your organizations stick to those principles and guidelines, right?

255
00:17:47,909 --> 00:17:51,830
so if we say that this is going to be the definition of done.

256
00:17:51,830 --> 00:17:55,190
we have to mean it, right? we have to actually say like, yes, this is the definition of done.

257
00:17:55,190 --> 00:17:58,070
you don't have documentation, so this isn't done.

258
00:17:58,070 --> 00:18:00,150
and we're not going to move on.

259
00:18:00,150 --> 00:18:03,750
and we're not gonna add work to your queue until that piece is done.

260
00:18:03,750 --> 00:18:07,830
but it really takes an organizational value system that says, you know, this is important.

261
00:18:07,830 --> 00:18:11,669
and we're not gonna move on until this piece is done.

262
00:18:11,669 --> 00:18:13,190
i think especially in the agile methodology.

263
00:18:13,190 --> 00:18:19,990
i think we give weight to words, but don't often put the systematic components behind that.

264
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:21,750
to make sure that these things stick.

265
00:18:21,750 --> 00:18:27,029
so it's one thing to say, you know, it's one thing to say we value automated tests.

266
00:18:27,039 --> 00:18:34,230
it's another thing to say you cannot merge a change without 98 test coverage, right?

267
00:18:34,230 --> 00:18:36,950
so that's the system sort of enforcing the values.

268
00:18:36,950 --> 00:18:38,710
that's being defined by the organization.

269
00:18:38,720 --> 00:18:42,390
so you know, listing documentation is done as part of the ticket works.

270
00:18:42,390 --> 00:18:45,029
as long as you have a systematic way to enforce that value.

271
00:18:45,039 --> 00:18:49,750
we start out talking about the problem of information hoarding.

272
00:18:49,750 --> 00:18:53,669
and we've been talking quite a lot about documentation.

273
00:18:53,669 --> 00:18:55,990
which is certainly not the only way to share information.

274
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:03,190
what are other ways that information can be discorded and shared among a team?

275
00:19:03,190 --> 00:19:07,110
you know, i think presentations are becoming extremely popular in organizations now.

276
00:19:07,110 --> 00:19:09,590
and some people prefer that, right?

277
00:19:09,590 --> 00:19:12,630
and the thing i like about presentations is there's so many different ways.

278
00:19:12,630 --> 00:19:15,750
that you can do it. you can do something as casual as like a lunch and learn.

279
00:19:15,760 --> 00:19:21,110
i know at centro, as an organization, you know, we would do these lightning talks during lunch break.

280
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:25,270
someone would just come in and spend five minutes just sort of talking about a thing.

281
00:19:25,270 --> 00:19:29,990
and you know, you may not get a complete understanding in a five-minute conversation.

282
00:19:29,990 --> 00:19:33,590
but it sparks the juices to initiate longer conversations, right?

283
00:19:33,590 --> 00:19:35,430
people begin to pull information, right?

284
00:19:35,430 --> 00:19:39,270
because that's another thing, how do you incite people to pull information?

285
00:19:39,270 --> 00:19:41,430
as opposed to it constantly having to be pushed.

286
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:46,070
so you know, that little five-minute talk might stir up a bunch of conversations.

287
00:19:46,070 --> 00:19:49,430
amongst engineers to say, hey, i want to learn a little bit more about this.

288
00:19:49,430 --> 00:19:53,510
and then the five-minute lightning talk turns into a 30-minute lunch and learn.

289
00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:56,950
with a bunch of people gathered around laptops sort of hacking away at things.

290
00:19:56,960 --> 00:20:00,310
so another nice thing about presentations is.

291
00:20:00,310 --> 00:20:04,390
especially in the age of zoom, for people that aren't super comfortable presenting in front of a crowd.

292
00:20:04,390 --> 00:20:07,430
you can pre-record it, right?

293
00:20:07,430 --> 00:20:11,110
you sit at home, and you go through a few iterations. you you nail down how you want the presentation to go.

294
00:20:11,110 --> 00:20:14,390
and then you throw it up on your confluence of your wiki site.

295
00:20:14,390 --> 00:20:17,990
and you say like, hey guys, you know, i just did a presentation on this.

296
00:20:17,990 --> 00:20:19,830
and would love for you to take a look at it, and give feedback.

297
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:24,710
that's another way to sort of disseminate information.

298
00:20:24,710 --> 00:20:28,710
so you know, i always say, you know, try to get creative with how you go about it.

299
00:20:28,710 --> 00:20:32,310
i think another great thing is training someone.

300
00:20:32,310 --> 00:20:34,470
that hasn't done, or hasn't worked with the particular system.

301
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:37,430
because not only does it enhance your knowledge of it.

302
00:20:37,430 --> 00:20:41,750
because whenever you have to teach it, it strengthens your understanding.

303
00:20:41,750 --> 00:20:44,070
but it is a great hands-on method for folks that, you know.

304
00:20:44,080 --> 00:20:49,430
may not absorb information great either through video or documentation.

305
00:20:49,430 --> 00:20:51,750
some people really need that sort of hands-on experience.

306
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:59,030
so i would say, do not be afraid of different ways to communicate documentation. or knowledge, i should say.

307
00:20:59,039 --> 00:21:04,710
primarily because different people respond differently to different modes of learning.

308
00:21:04,710 --> 00:21:09,990
and i think for so long we have placed a singular value on documentation.

309
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:15,350
written documentation, and haven't really explored the breadth of, you know, different learning options.

310
00:21:15,360 --> 00:21:17,909
a skill is something that you can do.

311
00:21:17,909 --> 00:21:20,950
and in some cases, if you tell me how to do something.

312
00:21:20,950 --> 00:21:25,510
i might say, yeah, i got that, i could do that.

313
00:21:25,510 --> 00:21:29,350
but in many cases i need to do it myself.

314
00:21:29,350 --> 00:21:34,070
you're going in a direction of instead of we send all the build tickets to brent.

315
00:21:34,070 --> 00:21:37,029
because he knows how the build works.

316
00:21:37,029 --> 00:21:40,951
we would intentionally send some of the tickets to other people on the team. who don't know how to do that.

317
00:21:40,960 --> 00:21:44,149
and then they would have to ask brent for help.

318
00:21:44,159 --> 00:21:47,830
and then skill would get shared in that way.

319
00:21:47,840 --> 00:21:49,909
absolutely.

320
00:21:49,909 --> 00:21:56,070
but then the other thing that you have to accompany with that is empowerment, right?

321
00:21:56,070 --> 00:21:57,830
so a thing that is commonly seen is, you might even have people.

322
00:21:57,830 --> 00:21:59,510
that are interested in learning how to do that.

323
00:21:59,520 --> 00:22:01,590
but simply don't have the access.

324
00:22:01,600 --> 00:22:04,870
and then it becomes an exercise in frustration.

325
00:22:04,870 --> 00:22:08,549
and people are like, you know, look like either i'm going to learn how to do this and support this.

326
00:22:08,549 --> 00:22:11,830
and i'm going to have the ability to do it, or i'm not.

327
00:22:11,830 --> 00:22:14,710
and i'm just going to sort of disengage. and continue to chuck everything over to brent.

328
00:22:14,720 --> 00:22:18,870
so i think there's a ton of value in finding those folks that are interested.

329
00:22:18,880 --> 00:22:23,029
and giving them the ability to do what they need to do.

330
00:22:23,029 --> 00:22:27,590
so i'll give you an example at centro at centro when i came on.

331
00:22:27,590 --> 00:22:30,390
the qa team sort of owned the cicd process.

332
00:22:30,390 --> 00:22:32,710
and there was one guy that was responsible for it.

333
00:22:32,710 --> 00:22:35,909
one guy, he was the only one that understood the build system.

334
00:22:35,909 --> 00:22:38,630
so when he left, it fell to my group.

335
00:22:38,630 --> 00:22:41,669
just because no one else was there to sort of catch it.

336
00:22:41,669 --> 00:22:43,430
and we were the only ones that really had access to the system.

337
00:22:43,440 --> 00:22:47,750
as people started getting more and more interested.

338
00:22:47,750 --> 00:22:49,350
in sort of defining and understanding the build process.

339
00:22:49,360 --> 00:22:56,149
they kept running into these hurdles where it's like, well, i can't log on to the jenkins worker slaves.

340
00:22:56,149 --> 00:22:57,669
to know, you know, what version of ruby actually got installed.

341
00:22:57,679 --> 00:22:59,270
or what library is installed.

342
00:22:59,280 --> 00:23:02,950
you know, how do i do this, how do i do that?

343
00:23:02,950 --> 00:23:05,029
so we said, how do we just sort of open this up?

344
00:23:05,029 --> 00:23:07,270
and give people more access to jenkins.

345
00:23:07,280 --> 00:23:09,590
give them more access to these nodes.

346
00:23:09,590 --> 00:23:11,110
and you know, it's a slow process.

347
00:23:11,110 --> 00:23:16,070
because there's obviously, you know, things that you have to be concerned about.

348
00:23:16,070 --> 00:23:19,990
but at the same time, we started to enable a couple of developers to say, hey.

349
00:23:19,990 --> 00:23:21,590
you can manage the jenkins plug-ins, right?

350
00:23:21,590 --> 00:23:25,669
because, you know, from an operations perspective, i i really don't care what jenkins plug-in to use.

351
00:23:25,679 --> 00:23:29,590
as long as they meet particular, you know, security requirements and things like that.

352
00:23:29,600 --> 00:23:35,110
i'm not going to be the one in the way dictating that. so with that access came a sense of ownership.

353
00:23:35,120 --> 00:23:41,430
and you know, now they're sort of leading the charge in how we need to re-architect cicd.

354
00:23:41,430 --> 00:23:44,470
but they're also claiming the ownership of it, right?

355
00:23:44,470 --> 00:23:48,870
so now that they are owners of it.

356
00:23:48,870 --> 00:23:51,750
they are much more apt to teach other engineers about it.

357
00:23:51,750 --> 00:23:54,230
right, they're getting their recruiting help.

358
00:23:54,230 --> 00:23:56,310
so that it's no longer just one or two people that understand it.

359
00:23:56,320 --> 00:24:00,149
so i think it's an important process to sort of foster that.

360
00:24:00,149 --> 00:24:04,470
but with that needs to come the ability to actually implement an effect change.

361
00:24:04,480 --> 00:24:08,230
the next one i want to address is culture by decree.

362
00:24:08,240 --> 00:24:11,190
start out with, what does that one mean?

363
00:24:11,200 --> 00:24:16,390
culture by decree is basically when you sit up and say, this is what our culture is.

364
00:24:16,400 --> 00:24:17,909
and you write it down.

365
00:24:17,909 --> 00:24:21,590
you might even get a nice plaque for it, right?

366
00:24:21,590 --> 00:24:23,190
and put it in the kitchen or in the break room.

367
00:24:23,190 --> 00:24:27,190
and it's got some really flowery language about how awesome your organization is.

368
00:24:27,200 --> 00:24:30,710
and then when you talk to people in the organization, they don't recognize it.

369
00:24:30,720 --> 00:24:33,110
they say, what company are you talking about?

370
00:24:33,110 --> 00:24:36,870
oh, that's us, yeah, no, i wouldn't have understood that.

371
00:24:36,870 --> 00:24:39,029
you know, enron is the common example, right?

372
00:24:39,029 --> 00:24:40,549
when you read enron's, you know, mission statement.

373
00:24:40,559 --> 00:24:43,669
you're like, that sounds like the kind of company i want to work for.

374
00:24:43,679 --> 00:24:45,830
that doesn't sound too good, really.

375
00:24:45,830 --> 00:24:48,070
why is that a problem?

376
00:24:48,080 --> 00:24:54,390
well it's a problem because, well for starters, the culture that you define, it doesn't exist, right?

377
00:24:54,390 --> 00:24:59,430
so then you run into this sort of like broken reality of.

378
00:24:59,430 --> 00:25:03,350
i think the organization is one way because i have it on a plaque.

379
00:25:03,360 --> 00:25:06,630
but in reality, the culture is toxic.

380
00:25:06,640 --> 00:25:12,870
and it is the creation, or it is the sort of the underpinnings of a lot of issues.

381
00:25:12,870 --> 00:25:16,549
that are reverberating throughout the organization, right?

382
00:25:16,549 --> 00:25:19,990
and i think especially as you get higher in the organization. and you start dealing with senior leadership.

383
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:23,669
if senior leadership thinks they have this great fantastic culture.

384
00:25:23,679 --> 00:25:27,110
they're not addressing any of the issues or problems.

385
00:25:27,110 --> 00:25:31,110
that are created by that mistaken reality. and as a result, things sort of sour.

386
00:25:31,120 --> 00:25:34,390
so what i mean by that is.

387
00:25:34,390 --> 00:25:38,470
if you think you have an organization that is open, that is transparent.

388
00:25:38,480 --> 00:25:41,669
that is friendly to unique and diverse voices.

389
00:25:41,679 --> 00:25:45,190
you may be surprised to find out that, you know, people are stifled.

390
00:25:45,190 --> 00:25:46,710
and you're not getting the best views.

391
00:25:46,710 --> 00:25:50,549
you're not getting the best opinions, because those people feel intimidated.

392
00:25:50,559 --> 00:25:55,190
you know, i think a more recent example of this outside of technology.

393
00:25:55,200 --> 00:26:00,310
was when in obama's latest book, a promised land.

394
00:26:00,310 --> 00:26:03,750
he talks about how he had inadvertently created a hostile environment for women.

395
00:26:03,760 --> 00:26:05,750
and didn't know it.

396
00:26:05,750 --> 00:26:07,510
he was unaware of it until people brought it to his attention.

397
00:26:07,520 --> 00:26:09,590
so just imagine the impact that that has.

398
00:26:09,600 --> 00:26:14,630
when you have an entire organization of women that, you know, don't feel comfortable.

399
00:26:14,640 --> 00:26:19,110
speaking out about particular things or problems. or, you know, even raising those viewpoints.

400
00:26:19,120 --> 00:26:22,310
so culture by decree is a dangerous trap.

401
00:26:22,320 --> 00:26:29,190
because you need to understand what your culture actually is, in order to fix it.

402
00:26:29,190 --> 00:26:32,870
is the problem that your culture doesn't match the mission statement on the plaque?

403
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:39,269
or is the problem, if you have a dysfunctional culture.

404
00:26:39,269 --> 00:26:43,029
regardless of whether it matches what you have written down? so it depends how you look at it.

405
00:26:43,029 --> 00:26:45,750
i think it's important that your culture and your actual mission statement match.

406
00:26:45,760 --> 00:26:50,710
only because that's what you're selling to potential employees, right?

407
00:26:50,710 --> 00:26:53,269
and there's never a scenario where if you have a great culture.

408
00:26:53,279 --> 00:26:55,029
you don't want to advertise that, right?

409
00:26:55,039 --> 00:26:58,870
everyone, no one is like, yeah, we've got a great culture. but we try to keep it down the low.

410
00:26:58,870 --> 00:27:01,990
we don't want anybody to really know about that. so it's important that they match.

411
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:05,590
it's especially important though when it's dysfunctional.

412
00:27:05,590 --> 00:27:07,830
for all the reasons that we have sort of already sort of talked about, right?

413
00:27:07,840 --> 00:27:14,710
so i would say, you want your culture and your mission statement or whatever to match.

414
00:27:14,720 --> 00:27:20,149
so that as you're out there recruiting and talking with people, you can lean on that.

415
00:27:20,149 --> 00:27:24,470
but more importantly, i think it's important that you have concrete ways.

416
00:27:24,480 --> 00:27:32,230
on how you can describe and talk about how that culture is sort of reinforced.

417
00:27:32,240 --> 00:27:38,710
because, so in technology we have a huge fight over talent, right?

418
00:27:38,720 --> 00:27:42,230
so we're either going to have an arms race and salaries.

419
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:47,590
or we're going to have to start talking up the intangibles of an organization.

420
00:27:47,600 --> 00:27:51,110
and every interview, and you're probably the same way, robert.

421
00:27:51,110 --> 00:27:55,430
any interview i've ever been on, the question comes up, what's the culture like?

422
00:27:55,430 --> 00:27:59,991
no one ever says, ah, the culture is kind of a dumpster fire, you know? no one ever says that.

423
00:27:59,991 --> 00:28:04,310
everyone talks about, you know, yeah, it's pretty solid, you know, this, that.

424
00:28:04,310 --> 00:28:07,190
but like, how does your organization ensure that that culture is happening?

425
00:28:07,200 --> 00:28:12,149
how is that cultural reinforcement happening?

426
00:28:12,149 --> 00:28:15,990
what are the sort of things built into the system that allow that to happen?

427
00:28:15,990 --> 00:28:19,669
so like for example at centro, you know, diversity, equity, inclusion.

428
00:28:19,669 --> 00:28:22,549
is, you know, one of our top goals for 2021, right?

429
00:28:22,549 --> 00:28:27,909
like most organizations, but how are we demonstrating that that's actually important?

430
00:28:27,919 --> 00:28:31,190
we can say oh, yeah, we're doing dei, that's great.

431
00:28:31,190 --> 00:28:35,029
but to be able to talk about the community groups that we've put together.

432
00:28:35,029 --> 00:28:38,870
to talk about, you know, how we're sort of funding those community groups and their initiatives.

433
00:28:38,880 --> 00:28:45,990
to talk about how we're putting goals and metrics around hiring, right?

434
00:28:45,990 --> 00:28:47,510
those are concrete things that are systematic.

435
00:28:47,510 --> 00:28:52,549
that we're doing, that sort of reinforces our cultural values. and what it is that we're trying to accomplish.

436
00:28:52,559 --> 00:28:56,870
so you know, culture by decree is sort of useless.

437
00:28:56,870 --> 00:28:59,190
we have to understand what is an organization doing.

438
00:28:59,190 --> 00:29:02,950
to make sure that those cultural values are operating in the organization.

439
00:29:02,950 --> 00:29:09,350
big part of the book is about the adoption of devops. would you say devops is a culture?

