00:29:30,549 --> 00:29:34,389
it's very easy for teams to sort of go in their silos.

446
00:29:34,389 --> 00:29:38,950
and come up with their own plans and missions. without coordinating with other groups.

447
00:29:38,950 --> 00:29:44,549
and then coming out and saying, here we are, this is what we're doing, right?

448
00:29:44,549 --> 00:29:49,110
but it is definitely a culture to default to thinking. who else do we need to involve?

449
00:29:49,120 --> 00:29:52,149
and how do we get their opinion, how do we get their viewpoint?

450
00:29:52,159 --> 00:29:59,269
so building that culture, you know, obviously it starts from the top, right? like, how do we, it starts with leaders, i should say.

451
00:29:59,279 --> 00:30:02,630
i shouldn't say it starts from the top. it helps when you have leaders in place.

452
00:30:02,640 --> 00:30:08,870
you can have individual contributors that have quite a bit of sway or respect in the organization.

453
00:30:08,870 --> 00:30:17,430
start to foster that behavior by saying, you know, oh, what does dev think about this, right? what does ops think about this? how do we get their input?

454
00:30:17,440 --> 00:30:23,830
especially in areas that, you know, may not be the normal purview of that team, right?

455
00:30:23,830 --> 00:30:25,430
we at central do it with interviews, right?

456
00:30:25,430 --> 00:30:27,269
like, i just did an interview yesterday.

457
00:30:27,269 --> 00:30:31,830
but we brought in a development engineer to interview an ops person. why? because we want their perspective.

458
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:35,750
we want to know how they feel they'll be working with this person.

459
00:30:35,760 --> 00:30:38,389
is it going to be easy to work with this person?

460
00:30:38,389 --> 00:30:43,190
does this person have the skills that a developer would expect an operations engineer to have?

461
00:30:43,190 --> 00:30:46,789
and those are perspectives that, you know, i think i could have.

462
00:30:46,789 --> 00:30:49,590
but you know, why risk it when i can actually just bring in a software engineer to do that?

463
00:30:49,600 --> 00:30:53,430
so i think that's all part of the sort of devops culture movement.

464
00:30:53,440 --> 00:30:58,630
and it's unfortunate that it gets tagged with something like specifically like devops.

465
00:30:58,640 --> 00:31:01,590
because it's really just good organizational practice.

466
00:31:01,590 --> 00:31:04,789
it just so happens that it is sort of had this genesis.

467
00:31:04,789 --> 00:31:07,350
with these two historically warring factions between dev and ops.

468
00:31:07,360 --> 00:31:12,710
but you know, the adoption of devsecops, devsec phenops, all this stuff, right?

469
00:31:12,710 --> 00:31:14,549
just sort of further proves the point that this is a universal problem.

470
00:31:14,559 --> 00:31:17,269
going back to the original question we had, right?

471
00:31:17,269 --> 00:31:19,909
where it's like, you know, it's the same problem.

472
00:31:19,919 --> 00:31:23,110
we're just recognizing patterns and applying the fix.

473
00:31:23,120 --> 00:31:26,549
if your organization has many of these anti-patterns.

474
00:31:26,549 --> 00:31:29,669
and you haven't adopted devops culture.

