00:31:34,389 --> 00:31:37,669
in order to succeed, for the organization to succeed?

477
00:31:37,669 --> 00:31:40,230
i firmly believe so, right?

478
00:31:40,230 --> 00:31:45,509
because like, if not, it becomes action without conviction, right? and if it's not built in to the culture.

479
00:31:45,519 --> 00:31:52,549
like, i don't know, like process without purpose feels empty, right?

480
00:31:52,549 --> 00:31:56,470
it's like, you know, when we talk about pull requests for example, right?

481
00:31:56,470 --> 00:32:02,870
the pull request process survives and is valued.

482
00:32:02,870 --> 00:32:08,071
because people understand what it is that we're trying to accomplish, right? it's not big brother, right?

483
00:32:08,071 --> 00:32:11,909
it's, we want to make sure that things are meeting standards.

484
00:32:11,909 --> 00:32:13,509
that the changes are being communicated.

485
00:32:13,509 --> 00:32:15,110
so multiple people have eyes on it.

486
00:32:15,110 --> 00:32:19,430
we recognize that a single developer can make a mistake that another developer might catch.

487
00:32:19,440 --> 00:32:21,990
we believe in the process.

488
00:32:21,990 --> 00:32:27,029
so pull requests typically aren't sort of like these hostile acts, right?

489
00:32:27,029 --> 00:32:32,630
now when you get into an organization where pro requests do meet resistance and animosity, right?

490
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:35,269
it's usually because they don't understand what it is.

491
00:32:35,269 --> 00:32:37,350
that the pull request is actually trying to accomplish.

492
00:32:37,360 --> 00:32:43,990
or they feel that it is not accomplishing what they've set out for it to accomplish.

493
00:32:43,990 --> 00:32:46,470
but when you go into an organization, you say, you know, polls requests are going to.

494
00:32:46,480 --> 00:32:50,389
allow us to better communicate with each other.

495
00:32:50,389 --> 00:32:52,549
it's going to allow us to move faster in some regards, right?

496
00:32:52,549 --> 00:32:55,110
as we can begin to sort of automate some things.

497
00:32:55,110 --> 00:32:58,070
because we understand how the process is going to flow.

498
00:32:58,070 --> 00:33:01,830
so we know that, oh, you know, this system can automatically check these things. these things, and these things.

499
00:33:01,840 --> 00:33:05,350
and that might help us move a little bit faster.

500
00:33:05,350 --> 00:33:11,590
so all that is to say, i think process and tools are great.

501
00:33:11,590 --> 00:33:13,509
but we still have to understand why it is we're doing what we're doing.

502
00:33:13,519 --> 00:33:17,909
and understand that these are just enablers of that mission, right?

503
00:33:17,919 --> 00:33:21,190
but if kubernetes one day was to implode.

504
00:33:21,200 --> 00:33:25,830
we still want the mission of developers having a bit more control.

505
00:33:25,840 --> 00:33:28,870
and consistency in their environments.

506
00:33:28,870 --> 00:33:32,470
so just because kubernetes has gone away doesn't mean we've abandoned that goal.

507
00:33:32,480 --> 00:33:36,310
we just find the next technology that's going to do it.

508
00:33:36,310 --> 00:33:38,950
big part of what you're saying there is.

