00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:12,069
one that stood out to me was alert fatigue.

535
00:35:12,069 --> 00:35:18,069
perhaps this time we could start with a story or an example illustrating that.

536
00:35:18,069 --> 00:35:20,790
oh man, alert fatigue, okay.

537
00:35:20,790 --> 00:35:24,630
so i'll give you an example from just this morning, actually.

538
00:35:24,630 --> 00:35:29,349
we're moving over a bunch of our synthetic checks to removing our synthetic checks.

539
00:35:29,349 --> 00:35:32,950
over to datadog for a number of environments that we've got, right?

540
00:35:32,950 --> 00:35:39,671
and one of our staging environments, basically we sleep it at night and wake it up in the morning. basically save money in aws.

541
00:35:39,680 --> 00:35:43,510
so that we're not spending compute time on a system that no one's using.

542
00:35:43,520 --> 00:35:46,710
so the synthetic check is supposed to stop and shut down.

543
00:35:46,720 --> 00:35:49,829
prior to us shutting down the environment.

544
00:35:49,829 --> 00:35:51,750
and then start back up after we start the environment back up.

545
00:35:51,760 --> 00:35:53,829
as we've been tinkering with that.

546
00:35:53,829 --> 00:35:57,750
we've been sort of ignoring the fact that the timing is a little off.

547
00:35:57,750 --> 00:36:03,190
so the synthetic alerts fire even though everything is fine, right?

548
00:36:03,200 --> 00:36:05,910
it's just a matter of timing.

549
00:36:05,910 --> 00:36:08,870
well, you know, a day of work becomes two days of work.

550
00:36:08,880 --> 00:36:10,710
becomes five days of work.

551
00:36:10,720 --> 00:36:14,470
then next thing you know, the synthetic alarms are firing.

552
00:36:14,470 --> 00:36:16,310
it's a real problem, but guess what, we've ignored it.

