00:36:16,320 --> 00:36:20,710
because it's like, oh, yeah, it's just that thing. and those synthetic alarms are just gonna fire.

554
00:36:20,720 --> 00:36:24,550
but no, lo and behold, you know, staging o2 was actually broken.

555
00:36:24,560 --> 00:36:26,310
it's actually down.

556
00:36:26,310 --> 00:36:29,670
and you know, we wasted a ton of time because you know.

557
00:36:29,670 --> 00:36:34,069
because of the alert fatigue from the synthetic checks, we had ignored it.

558
00:36:34,069 --> 00:36:37,670
so it's important to sort of stay diligent about the alerts that go off.

559
00:36:37,680 --> 00:36:45,829
primarily because it can be so easy to ignore and forget.

560
00:36:45,829 --> 00:36:49,829
and then suddenly, not only is it causing a lot of anxiety on your on-call engineers.

561
00:36:49,829 --> 00:36:53,270
but it's also eliminating the usefulness of the alert.

562
00:36:53,280 --> 00:36:58,870
because the purpose of it is to inform someone that there's a problem. so that they can take action.

563
00:36:58,880 --> 00:37:03,109
if they're not going to take action right away, then you know, the alert has sort of failed its purpose.

564
00:37:03,119 --> 00:37:07,589
because it's still depending on, you know, some qa engineer going in.

565
00:37:07,589 --> 00:37:09,270
and saying, hey, staging o2 is not available.

566
00:37:09,270 --> 00:37:12,391
and it's like, oh, oops, yeah, we got an alert on that. and didn't do anything about it.

567
00:37:12,400 --> 00:37:17,109
so you could have over alerting or under alerting.

568
00:37:17,109 --> 00:37:19,270
and their costs either way.

569
00:37:19,270 --> 00:37:20,870
how do you strike a balance?

570
00:37:20,880 --> 00:37:28,470
so your mileage may vary with my answer, i'm gonna put that out there now, all right?

571
00:37:28,470 --> 00:37:31,670
but i would say, i tend to focus.

572
00:37:31,670 --> 00:37:35,990
i would rather be five minutes of late to an alert that's real.

573
00:37:35,990 --> 00:37:41,910
than to respond five times to an alert that's bogus, right?

574
00:37:41,910 --> 00:37:46,710
so as a result, a lot of the alerting that we have is set up.

575
00:37:46,710 --> 00:37:50,550
to know with the level of certainty that this is an actual problem.

576
00:37:50,550 --> 00:37:55,030
because now when i wake you up, you say, oh, there's something actually going on.

577
00:37:55,040 --> 00:37:58,470
versus getting the alert, looking at it, and then going back to sleep.

578
00:37:58,480 --> 00:38:00,230
and i think we've all had that scenario.

579
00:38:00,240 --> 00:38:05,030
where you've got that alert that has a funky pattern.

580
00:38:05,030 --> 00:38:06,550
so when you get it, you don't respond right away.

581
00:38:06,560 --> 00:38:08,230
you wait for the second alert.

582
00:38:08,230 --> 00:38:10,150
and then you go, oh, okay, this is real.

583
00:38:10,160 --> 00:38:12,630
now you're reacting, right?

584
00:38:12,630 --> 00:38:16,150
i don't know that there's value in that.

