but this alert is sort of canonized.

661
00:42:40,560 --> 00:42:45,670
with this mission statement around the previous alert.

662
00:42:45,670 --> 00:42:48,790
and it's like, well, you know, this is the thing that helps us prevent issue xyz.

663
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:54,390
when in reality, it's like, well, you know, it might detect that situation.

664
00:42:54,390 --> 00:42:58,790
but it also detects 20 other situations that aren't really helpful or useful to us.

665
00:42:58,800 --> 00:43:04,150
so how do we remove that? and people have a lot of emotion around that.

666
00:43:04,150 --> 00:43:06,309
a lot of fear, honestly.

667
00:43:06,309 --> 00:43:07,829
a lot of times it comes from management, right?

668
00:43:07,829 --> 00:43:12,630
because management never wants to question, you know, well, why didn't we know that this was a problem?

669
00:43:12,640 --> 00:43:17,990
and the reality, no one wants to hear the nightmare.

670
00:43:17,990 --> 00:43:20,309
that there are so many different ways that we can fail.

671
00:43:20,309 --> 00:43:24,309
that we're probably not going to catch them all, right?

672
00:43:24,309 --> 00:43:30,151
so we have to have this balance between, you know, understanding our systems and alerting properly. and burning our staff out.

673
00:43:30,160 --> 00:43:35,750
because they're constantly reacting to alarms that aren't helpful and or useful.

674
00:43:35,750 --> 00:43:39,511
that's another question that comes up at least in the op space. what's on call like?

675
00:43:39,520 --> 00:43:42,630
every interview i've ever done, what's the on call like?

676
00:43:42,640 --> 00:43:46,069
is it a lot, is it a little, how often are you paged?

677
00:43:46,079 --> 00:43:49,829
and you know, when people hear our philosophy that resonates with them.

678
00:43:49,839 --> 00:43:53,510
because everyone has been on the other side of the coin.

679
00:43:53,510 --> 00:43:55,910
so, where you may be going with this, and tell me this is is.

680
00:43:55,910 --> 00:44:02,230
what you're saying is, you might like to have every possible thing that can go wrong catalog.

681
00:44:02,240 --> 00:44:05,510
and have a run book that you link to in the alert.

682
00:44:05,520 --> 00:44:07,510
but that's not really possible.

683
00:44:07,510 --> 00:44:11,829
you need to draw a line between routine failure modes.

684
00:44:11,829 --> 00:44:15,430
and at some point you have people with skills.

685
00:44:15,430 --> 00:44:18,550
and you're going to punt it to the on-call person.

686
00:44:18,550 --> 00:44:20,470
say, you got to use your skills and figure out what's going on.

687
00:44:20,480 --> 00:44:23,270
because we can't anticipate everything.

688
00:44:23,280 --> 00:44:25,510
exactly. you're not going to anticipate everything.

689
00:44:25,520 --> 00:44:28,550
and the minute you did anticipate everything.

690
00:44:28,550 --> 00:44:33,910
the next release and validates it, or creates a new failure mode, right? so if you're in a high release environment.

691
00:44:33,910 --> 00:44:36,790
you know, it's just a matter of time before a new failure mode gets introduced.

692
00:44:36,790 --> 00:44:38,390
that you weren't prepared for.

693
00:44:38,400 --> 00:44:43,510
so you have to have a process in place for, you know, identifying.

694
00:44:43,510 --> 00:44:45,030
you know, sort of routine failures.

695
00:44:45,030 --> 00:44:48,790
you have to have a process in place for identifying things that are worth investigation, right?

696
00:44:48,790 --> 00:44:51,829
so here's another thing that we talk about in the book.

697
00:44:51,829 --> 00:44:56,309
is not every alert has to wake someone up, right?

698
00:44:56,309 --> 00:45:03,271
so if you have a situation where you're like, you know, i think this particular combination of circumstances is curious, right? turn it into an email alert.

699
00:45:03,271 --> 00:45:06,550
and then someone can look at it and investigate it.

700
00:45:06,550 --> 00:45:10,230
and decide, oh, yes, this is something that should be elevated to a page.

701
00:45:10,230 --> 00:45:12,630
or no, this is something that should be continue to be a low-level alert.

702
00:45:12,640 --> 00:45:16,710
i think making that distinction is extremely important as well.

703
00:45:16,720 --> 00:45:21,910
because it gives you an opportunity to sort of get a sense.

704
00:45:21,910 --> 00:45:23,829
of what the alert volume will look like, right?

705
00:45:23,829 --> 00:45:31,030
so if you're, and the minute you say the minute you create your email rule. to filter out that alert, and put it in a folder.

706
00:45:31,040 --> 00:45:35,349
that means it's not useful. and, uh, you know, it's time to get rid of it.

707
00:45:35,359 --> 00:45:38,710
we're talking in different ways about where do you set the bar?

708
00:45:38,720 --> 00:45:42,870
what's actionable, what's urgent? when you're launching a new system.

709
00:45:42,880 --> 00:45:47,270
you don't have the experience of what is normal or abnormal.

710
00:45:47,270 --> 00:45:51,750
how do you go about setting up a decent set of initial alerts?

711
00:45:51,760 --> 00:45:54,950
so a technique that i've used.

712
00:45:54,950 --> 00:45:56,710
i actually stole from the manufacturing industry.

713
00:45:56,720 --> 00:45:58,950
and my wife who's an industrial engineer.

714
00:45:58,960 --> 00:46:02,550
but in manufacturing they use this thing called failure modes effects analysis.

715
00:46:02,560 --> 00:46:07,589
and the goal of that is essentially to map out this process that you're doing, right?

716
00:46:07,599 --> 00:46:10,870
in manufacturing, it's it's actual physical construction of things.

717
00:46:10,880 --> 00:46:17,510
but for us software, it's, you know, various handoffs, interactions with apis, databases, things like that.

718
00:46:17,520 --> 00:46:19,910
so you map this process out.

719
00:46:19,910 --> 00:46:21,430
and it could be pretty exhaustive.

720
00:46:21,440 --> 00:46:29,109
but you then say, all right, what could go wrong in this? where are the things that could break?

721
00:46:29,119 --> 00:46:31,030
and you start to highlight those out.

722
00:46:31,040 --> 00:46:35,910
once you've highlighted those out, you rank them by three different categories, right?

723
00:46:35,910 --> 00:46:41,510
the likelihood that it's going to occur, right?

724
00:46:41,510 --> 00:46:46,150
the severity, or the impact of if it occurs, how bad is it. and current severity and detection.

725
00:46:46,160 --> 00:46:49,829
what is the likelihood that if it happened, we would detect it prior?

726
00:46:49,829 --> 00:46:53,910
or we could detect it prior to a customer being notified.

727
00:46:53,910 --> 00:46:55,990
you take those three numbers, and you multiply them together.

728
00:46:56,000 --> 00:46:59,670
and that gives you your risk priority number.

729
00:46:59,670 --> 00:47:02,710
and from there, you can sort of work the risk priority number.

730
00:47:02,710 --> 00:47:06,550
to develop different techniques for either lowering any one of those options.

731
00:47:06,560 --> 00:47:11,829
so you might say, okay, this thing has a pretty likely high occurrence rating.

732
00:47:11,829 --> 00:47:14,150
and the severity is pretty bad.

733
00:47:14,150 --> 00:47:17,190
what can we do to mitigate the surveillance number?

734
00:47:17,190 --> 00:47:22,470
or what can we do to better detect that this incident or situation has occurred?

735
00:47:22,480 --> 00:47:27,750
so through that, you end up creating a series of either work items.

736
00:47:27,750 --> 00:47:29,670
for development to, you know, come up with a more robust solution.

737
00:47:29,680 --> 00:47:33,510
or it may be monitoring to be able to lower the detection number.

738
00:47:33,520 --> 00:47:35,990
to a point where the risk is lower, right?

739
00:47:36,000 --> 00:47:40,069
because, all right, we've detected this before a customer has done anything. and we can do that.

740
00:47:40,079 --> 00:47:45,030
so we've used that to drive a lot of monitoring, right?

741
00:47:45,030 --> 00:47:49,030
because a lot of times there are handoffs between systems or data transfers.

742
00:47:49,030 --> 00:47:51,670
and you don't think about it when you're designing it or building it.

743
00:47:51,680 --> 00:47:56,230
but now that you recognize that this is an important interaction between these systems.

744
00:47:56,230 --> 00:47:58,790
you say, okay, we need to track this somehow, right?

745
00:47:58,790 --> 00:48:02,549
so if i send a message, i should receive an acknowledgement.

746
00:48:02,549 --> 00:48:05,829
or i should, i should detect the output of that message that i sent.

747
00:48:05,829 --> 00:48:07,910
so that i know it was actually completed.

748
00:48:07,920 --> 00:48:11,990
so we've used that in a number of organizations.

749
00:48:11,990 --> 00:48:15,109
and it really helps drive the creative juices for people to think like.

750
00:48:15,109 --> 00:48:18,390
oh yeah, we want to make sure that this step actually completed.

751
00:48:18,390 --> 00:48:23,990
because it's huge if it doesn't. again, going back to what we said originally. you're not going to be able to catalog everything.

752
00:48:24,000 --> 00:48:25,910
but now that you have a risk priority number.

753
00:48:25,920 --> 00:48:29,510
you at least understand what are the top five things you should address.

754
00:48:29,520 --> 00:48:33,990
and then you can constantly keep going that back to that backlog, right? let's go back to the backlog.

755
00:48:33,990 --> 00:48:37,270
what else can we, you know, solve or lower the detection number on?

756
00:48:37,280 --> 00:48:40,950
through monitoring and things of that nature.

757
00:48:40,950 --> 00:48:42,710
you also end up getting a really robust understanding of the system.

758
00:48:42,720 --> 00:48:44,309
because every time i've done this.

759
00:48:44,309 --> 00:48:47,030
people that think they're experts end up opening their laptop.

760
00:48:47,030 --> 00:48:49,750
and looking through code to understand exactly what's happening.

761
00:48:49,760 --> 00:48:51,430
jeff, we have a bit of time left.

762
00:48:51,430 --> 00:48:55,430
i want to delve into one more of these anti-patterns.

763
00:48:55,430 --> 00:48:57,589
it's called the empty toolbox.

764
00:48:57,599 --> 00:49:06,390
and i'm going to describe that as a tendency for organizations to under invest in tooling. is that a fair description?

765
00:49:06,400 --> 00:49:10,391
yep, very fair. what drives that?

766
00:49:10,400 --> 00:49:15,190
sometimes it's driven by a clear lack of ownership.

767
00:49:15,190 --> 00:49:18,790
in terms of who should be managing, creating that tooling, right?

768
00:49:18,790 --> 00:49:21,750
so every company i've been at has always talked about.

769
00:49:21,750 --> 00:49:23,270
we should create an internal tools team.

770
00:49:23,280 --> 00:49:26,470
so that we have all of these things that we need.

771
00:49:26,480 --> 00:49:31,430
i think some of it has been this over reliance on open source, for lack of a better term.

772
00:49:31,430 --> 00:49:33,109
and i'm going to get some smoke here, i'm sure.

773
00:49:33,119 --> 00:49:36,870
but you know, we tend to say like, well, if there's no free open source solution.

774
00:49:36,880 --> 00:49:41,589
then it's not worth paying for. and some things are worth paying for, right?

775
00:49:41,599 --> 00:49:45,510
even open source. so you know, you get into this weird thing where it's like.

776
00:49:45,520 --> 00:49:48,870
you know, we're not going to invest the time and energy to build these tools.

777
00:49:48,880 --> 00:49:52,630
and because there's nothing free on the marketplace, we're not going to buy anything.

778
00:49:52,640 --> 00:49:55,430
so we live in this sort of quagmire.

779
00:49:55,430 --> 00:50:02,870
that really just sort of expresses itself predominantly through, you know, wasted engineer time, wasted developer time.

780
00:50:02,870 --> 00:50:07,990
but if organizations aren't sophisticated in measuring and monitoring these soft costs.

781
00:50:08,000 --> 00:50:11,510
you know, the pain point sort of become invisible.

782
00:50:11,520 --> 00:50:15,510
so i'm trying to think of a recent example that i can give you.

783
00:50:15,520 --> 00:50:17,910
okay, something we just did last week.

784
00:50:17,910 --> 00:50:26,549
so we have a process that creates dynamically creates an im user for an s3 bucket for customers, right?

785
00:50:26,549 --> 00:50:32,309
in testing, what would happen is there would be some weird confluence of scenarios.

786
00:50:32,309 --> 00:50:38,710
that would result in an environment creating, but not destroying that record, that user.

787
00:50:38,710 --> 00:50:41,510
so then when they needed to retest the process with the same environment.

788
00:50:41,510 --> 00:50:43,589
it would suddenly blow up.

789
00:50:43,589 --> 00:50:47,589
that would then generate a ticket to my team.

790
00:50:47,589 --> 00:50:52,150
because my team was the only team that had the access to go through and delete that user.

791
00:50:52,150 --> 00:50:53,670
so now the qa engineer is stuck.

792
00:50:53,680 --> 00:50:55,750
she has to create a ticket.

793
00:50:55,750 --> 00:51:02,390
she's got to wait for my team to actually get the ticket, acknowledge it, do the work.

794
00:51:02,390 --> 00:51:05,750
the work is probably 90 seconds, maybe 60, right?

795
00:51:05,750 --> 00:51:09,670
but meanwhile, they've been blocked for, you know, five or six hours.

796
00:51:09,670 --> 00:51:14,150
because queue time is clearly the largest portion of the process.

797
00:51:14,160 --> 00:51:15,829
it's not the actual work.

798
00:51:15,829 --> 00:51:18,230
it's waiting around for someone to actually get to the work.

799
00:51:18,240 --> 00:51:25,030
so because we have such a focus on, you know, internal tooling and internal empowerment.

800
00:51:25,030 --> 00:51:30,470
we said, let's just write a script that people can do this themselves, right? let's offload this, and you know, allow users to do that.

801
00:51:30,480 --> 00:51:33,910
oh, but what if they delete a user in production?

802
00:51:33,910 --> 00:51:37,030
okay, we'll restrict it specifically to the pre-production account, right?

803
00:51:37,040 --> 00:51:41,270
so if they delete a, erroneously deleted user in pre-prod.

804
00:51:41,270 --> 00:51:43,350
nobody really cares, right? so here, here's your command.

805
00:51:43,350 --> 00:51:50,870
you know, now this six hour window of dead time is literally five minutes. they don't even need to create a ticket.

806
00:51:50,870 --> 00:51:53,190
you know, they just do what they need to do.

807
00:51:53,190 --> 00:51:58,549
and then the nice thing is with that automation, we can then hook that into other processes in the future, right?

808
00:51:58,549 --> 00:52:00,309
so maybe one day we do want to jira ticket.

809
00:52:00,319 --> 00:52:03,190
we don't need to have the qa engineer to create it.

810
00:52:03,190 --> 00:52:05,829
we can just have the automation created right before it actually does its task.

811
00:52:05,839 --> 00:52:08,630
so that's the sort of tool box that needs to get filled up.

812
00:52:08,640 --> 00:52:10,630
another great example is.

813
00:52:10,630 --> 00:52:15,430
we were noticing that we would get these requests to run ad hoc scripts.

814
00:52:15,430 --> 00:52:17,349
and because we were the only ones with ssh access in production.

815
00:52:17,359 --> 00:52:18,870
it would come in through a ticket.

816
00:52:18,880 --> 00:52:21,910
you know, someone would have to approve it and the developer side.

817
00:52:21,910 --> 00:52:25,030
and then an engineer from my team would have to log in and run this script.

818
00:52:25,030 --> 00:52:27,030
and we said, this is dumb.

819
00:52:27,030 --> 00:52:29,190
we're adding zero value to this.

820
00:52:29,190 --> 00:52:30,710
why don't we invest time and turn this into a jira workflow?

821
00:52:30,720 --> 00:52:35,990
so now people can attach a ticket to jira. they can create a jira ticket, they can create a pr.

822
00:52:36,000 --> 00:52:37,750
someone has to approve the pr.

823
00:52:37,750 --> 00:52:39,430
someone has to approve the jira ticket.

824
00:52:39,440 --> 00:52:43,030
and then the automation downloads the script and executes it for them.

825
00:52:43,040 --> 00:52:47,109
and now they're able to move through these things so much faster than they were before.

826
00:52:47,119 --> 00:52:52,710
where it might take a day, a day and a half, two days. for my team to actually free up to run this.

827
00:52:52,720 --> 00:52:56,630
you know, now they can do it all within 15 minutes. and it's all within their team.

828
00:52:56,640 --> 00:53:01,750
and everyone that's part of the process is adding value, right?

829
00:53:01,750 --> 00:53:03,270
the engineers that are reviewing it are adding value.

830
00:53:03,280 --> 00:53:07,990
the person that submitted the ticket is adding value. you don't have ops just sitting around.

831
00:53:07,990 --> 00:53:11,750
you know, just typing like, oh, i guess so, we're going to have to run this. because engineers said so.

832
00:53:11,760 --> 00:53:15,190
and i don't have the expertise to, you know, comment on your script.

833
00:53:15,190 --> 00:53:16,790
on the environment that you built.

834
00:53:16,800 --> 00:53:23,510
so you know, why is so much energy and focus being put on my approval?

835
00:53:23,510 --> 00:53:25,030
when i'm not really adding that much value to the process.

836
00:53:25,040 --> 00:53:31,430
we start out with the premise, organizations may under invest in tooling.

837
00:53:31,440 --> 00:53:36,069
you point out certainly there is a cost, especially if you buy tools.

838
00:53:36,069 --> 00:53:39,349
but creating your own tools is costly as well.

839
00:53:39,359 --> 00:53:45,270
and i want to focus a bit on cost of non-automation.

840
00:53:45,270 --> 00:53:50,150
you need to understand cause of non-automation to decide if you're under investing in tooling.

841
00:53:50,150 --> 00:53:54,630
you did mention queue time and time to do the actual work.

842
00:53:54,640 --> 00:53:59,349
are there other costs of non-automation that should be taken into account?

843
00:53:59,359 --> 00:54:02,549
in trying to assess are you under investing?

844
00:54:02,559 --> 00:54:06,710
i think repeatability is probably the biggest thing.

845
00:54:06,710 --> 00:54:10,390
is the ability to do an action consistently and follow a complete process.

846
00:54:10,400 --> 00:54:15,270
and you know, we always sort of assign that specifically to the technical task at hand.

847
00:54:15,280 --> 00:54:20,710
but it could be all of the supporting processes around it too, right?

848
00:54:20,710 --> 00:54:24,230
so like, let's say for example, we say we always want a jira ticket.

849
00:54:24,230 --> 00:54:28,630
that's been approved before we run command x.

850
00:54:28,630 --> 00:54:30,390
if we do that manually, you know, ken might message me and say like.

851
00:54:30,400 --> 00:54:32,790
hey, it's saturday, i really need to get this out.

852
00:54:32,800 --> 00:54:36,470
can you do this for me? and i'll follow up with a ticket on monday. yeah, sure, ken, no problem.

853
00:54:36,480 --> 00:54:39,430
but then the ticket doesn't come monday, and we forget about it.

854
00:54:39,430 --> 00:54:42,230
then there's an audit, and someone says, well, where's the jira ticket associated with this?

855
00:54:42,230 --> 00:54:46,231
because your process says you do this. oops, right?

856
00:54:46,231 --> 00:54:47,910
and i think that happens a lot in organizations, right?

857
00:54:47,920 --> 00:54:53,190
because you know, as much as we all recognize the need for tickets and things like that.

858
00:54:53,190 --> 00:54:58,870
we also all sort of have an adversity to bureaucracy. now if you automate that, guess what?

859
00:54:58,870 --> 00:55:02,390
there's no way around that, because the automation is going to do it for you, right?

860
00:55:02,390 --> 00:55:05,990
so you're always going to have that consistency.

861
00:55:05,990 --> 00:55:07,670
that also goes down to actually executing the command, right?

862
00:55:07,680 --> 00:55:13,430
people feel safe, they're like, oh, well, before we do this command in production.

863
00:55:13,430 --> 00:55:14,951
we always run it in staging first. it's like, oh, okay.

864
00:55:14,960 --> 00:55:18,630
and there's a human typing that in staging, right?

865
00:55:18,630 --> 00:55:25,430
and are you sure that they type the exact same thing that they were going to type in production? while they copy and paste it. are you sure they highlighted the entire line?

866
00:55:25,440 --> 00:55:27,190
that they accidentally miss a line break.

867
00:55:27,200 --> 00:55:32,230
and suddenly, you know, the dash dash safety flag is missing. these things happen all the time.

868
00:55:32,240 --> 00:55:36,230
so i think, you know, thinking about repeatability.

869
00:55:36,230 --> 00:55:41,670
and the risk of something not being repeated exactly as is.

870
00:55:41,670 --> 00:55:43,350
needs to weigh in and factor in as well. now on the other side of that.

871
00:55:43,350 --> 00:55:47,510
you also have the upkeep and the maintenance of automation, right?

872
00:55:47,520 --> 00:55:50,710
because as things change, as processes change.

873
00:55:50,720 --> 00:55:57,190
any software that you ever write is probably going to have a bug or two in it. so you need to think about that cost as well.

874
00:55:57,200 --> 00:56:01,589
one of the things we talk about is, how frequently are you actually doing this task?

875
00:56:01,599 --> 00:56:05,750
if it's something that you're doing like once a year.

876
00:56:05,750 --> 00:56:07,670
automating it may not be the best solution.

877
00:56:07,670 --> 00:56:11,830
only because who knows what the environment looks like a year from now, right? it could be completely different.

878
00:56:11,839 --> 00:56:15,430
and the code makes all these assumptions that were valid a year ago.

879
00:56:15,440 --> 00:56:17,750
but are now suddenly invalid.

880
00:56:17,750 --> 00:56:19,910
and now becomes this huge quagmire.

881
00:56:19,910 --> 00:56:23,349
because, you know, the script started and failed halfway through or something like that.

882
00:56:23,359 --> 00:56:26,630
so there are definitely, you know, things that you need to sort of weigh.

883
00:56:26,630 --> 00:56:30,711
when you're looking at automating. cost, repeatability, frequency.

884
00:56:30,720 --> 00:56:33,430
i think are some of the big ones.

885
00:56:33,440 --> 00:56:36,230
sure.

886
00:56:36,230 --> 00:56:40,309
the frequency issue, you could turn that around and say.

887
00:56:40,309 --> 00:56:42,230
there is something that has value to do, but no one ever does it.

888
00:56:42,240 --> 00:56:44,309
because it's such a big pain.

889
00:56:44,309 --> 00:56:52,231
and if it were automated, you might do it more often and get some value out of that, right? and you know, for that i would say.

890
00:56:52,240 --> 00:56:56,470
the script, the automation is less about the frequency which you do it today.

891
00:56:56,480 --> 00:56:59,829
just the frequency that it would need to be executed regularly.

892
00:56:59,829 --> 00:57:05,510
so like, i remember working at a shop where we had talked about doing scripting.

893
00:57:05,510 --> 00:57:08,470
completely automated database failovers, right?

894
00:57:08,470 --> 00:57:11,430
and then when we finally got around to doing it, it was probably a year and a half later.

895
00:57:11,440 --> 00:57:16,870
when we actually needed it. no one had confidence in the script, all right?

896
00:57:16,870 --> 00:57:18,950
no one felt confident like, yeah, let's just run this and it'll fail over.

897
00:57:18,960 --> 00:57:23,270
granted, the same people that wrote it were in the room.

898
00:57:23,270 --> 00:57:26,069
but everyone's like, no, we let's do this by hand.

899
00:57:26,069 --> 00:57:31,430
so that's more of the frequency thing that i was referring to. i completely agree. like once you have something automated.

900
00:57:31,440 --> 00:57:37,829
the frequency at which people do things increases, sometimes for the worse, right?

901
00:57:37,829 --> 00:57:41,990
because what we've seen with like our automated scripts execution framework is that.

902
00:57:42,000 --> 00:57:47,270
now instead of fixing some long-term issues.

903
00:57:47,280 --> 00:57:51,670
we just have one-off scripts to correct the occurrence, right?

904
00:57:51,670 --> 00:57:54,150
and it's like, well, this is the same problem that keeps happening.

905
00:57:54,150 --> 00:57:56,309
and because we've made it so easy.

906
00:57:56,309 --> 00:58:01,349
just to run this script that everyone knows about to correct the situation.

907
00:58:01,349 --> 00:58:05,510
we opt for that as opposed to, well, how do we prevent the situation from happening period?

908
00:58:05,520 --> 00:58:11,990
so in that scenario, the friction of the manual process would actually be a benefit.

909
00:58:11,990 --> 00:58:16,950
because it then says, you know what, it's a pain in the butt every time we've got to do this. we just need to solve this problem.

910
00:58:16,960 --> 00:58:23,829
but now it's like, oh yeah, you know, because the other thing is the fix isn't concentrated in one person, right?

911
00:58:23,829 --> 00:58:25,510
so if you've got a group of 40 developers.

912
00:58:25,510 --> 00:58:27,829
and each developer experiences it once a month, right?

913
00:58:27,829 --> 00:58:34,309
the chances of someone saying, this is worth tackling in a bigger scenario, is deeply lessened.

914
00:58:34,309 --> 00:58:36,150
jeff, we're getting close to end of time.

915
00:58:36,150 --> 00:58:37,829
are there any key takeaways we haven't covered.

916
00:58:37,829 --> 00:58:39,910
that you want to pass on to our listeners?

917
00:58:39,920 --> 00:58:46,150
i think the key takeaway is really just, you know, focus on your people, right? focus on the communication between teams.

918
00:58:46,150 --> 00:58:48,710
you know, when people talk about devops.

919
00:58:48,710 --> 00:58:52,230
it's very easy to get wrapped up in tools and all the fancy whiz banging stuff.

920
00:58:52,240 --> 00:58:55,589
but focus on your people, focus on your processes.

921
00:58:55,589 --> 00:58:58,950
and i think you're going to get huge dividends.

922
00:58:58,950 --> 00:59:02,069
just by enhancing people's ability to communicate and get their jobs done.

923
